  "notes": [
    "It has its own baud rate generator. A 1.8432 MHz crystal on XTLI and XTLO gives every standard rate from 50 to 19200 baud with no divider maths.",
    "Only four registers, selected by RS0 and RS1: data, status, command and control. Wire those to CPU A0 and A1.",
    "The CMOS W65C51N sold today has a well-known transmitter bug: the transmit-empty flag always reads 1, so you cannot poll it. The usual workaround is a fixed delay per character. The original NMOS 6551 does not have this problem.",
    "IRQ is open drain, so it shares one interrupt line with the VIA and anything else through a single pull-up.",
    "The modem control lines are real: RTS, CTS, DTR, DSR and DCD are all brought out, which is enough for proper hardware flow control.",
    "Output levels are TTL, not RS-232. A MAX232 or equivalent sits between this and a real serial port; for a modern USB adapter, wire TxD and RxD straight across.",
    "Pair it with a 6522 VIA and a 6502 and you have the classic three-chip computer."
  ],
